# PART 5: DAILY PLANNER

Build a complete daily scheduling system.

The user must be able to create a detailed schedule for any date.

## A. Time-based planning

Support:

* 24-hour clock
* Configurable day start and end
* Time slots of 15, 30, or 60 minutes
* Custom task durations
* Scheduled breaks
* Flexible, unscheduled tasks
* Overlapping schedule detection
* Rescheduling
* Recurring schedule templates

The default day can run from 06:00 to 23:00, but the user must be able to change it.

Display the schedule as a chronological timeline.

If practical, provide both timeline and agenda views.

## B. Schedule creation

Every scheduled activity should support:

* Title
* Description
* Date
* Start time
* End time
* Category
* Priority
* Estimated duration
* Linked task, if applicable
* Linked habit, if applicable
* Linked goal, if applicable
* Notes
* Status

Calculate duration automatically from the start and end times.

Warn the user when scheduled activities overlap.

Do not silently overwrite another scheduled activity.

## C. Planned versus actual time

For each activity, track:

* Planned start
* Planned end
* Planned duration
* Actual start
* Actual end
* Actual duration
* Time variance
* Completion status

Calculate:

Time variance = Actual duration - Planned duration

A positive value means more time was spent than planned.

A negative value means less time was spent than planned.

Allow the user to record actual duration manually if a timer was not used.

## D. Daily planning workflow

Support the following workflow:

1. Create tasks.
2. Set task priorities.
3. Assign tasks to time slots.
4. Review schedule conflicts.
5. Start the day.
6. Track task execution.
7. Mark tasks completed or skipped.
8. Record reasons for unfinished work when desired.
9. Review daily performance.

Make the workflow quick and intuitive.

Do not force the user to fill in every optional field.

## E. Recurring schedules

Support recurring activities such as:

* Daily exercise
* Daily study
* Weekly review
* Monthly planning

Support appropriate recurrence rules, including selected weekdays.

When generating future scheduled occurrences, prevent accidental duplication.

Editing one occurrence should not unexpectedly modify the entire recurring series.

Provide options to edit:

* This occurrence
* This and future occurrences
* The entire series

Implement these controls only when the underlying recurrence model can support them safely.

