# PART 11: AUTOMATED ANALYTICS ENGINE

This is one of the most important parts of the application.

Build a centralized analytics engine using real saved data.

Do not hardcode chart values, sample performance percentages, or fake productivity scores.

Use reusable, testable calculation functions.

All reports must be calculated from the same underlying records so that the dashboard and analytics screens agree.

## A. Daily analytics

For each day, calculate:

* Total scheduled tasks
* Completed tasks
* Incomplete tasks
* Overdue tasks
* Task completion rate
* Scheduled minutes
* Actual tracked minutes
* Time variance
* Schedule adherence
* Habit completion rate
* Goal progress
* Productivity score
* Comparison with the previous day

Define every metric precisely.

For example:

Task completion rate =
Completed eligible tasks / Total eligible planned tasks × 100

Do not include cancelled tasks in the denominator.

Define how skipped tasks and overdue tasks affect the metric, and use the same rules throughout the application.

Schedule adherence should be based on actual execution relative to planned activities, not simply on the percentage of tasks completed.

Do not assume a scheduled task was performed merely because its time slot has passed.

## B. Weekly analytics

Calculate:

* Tasks completed during the week
* Weekly task completion rate
* Planned versus actual hours
* Habit consistency
* Goal progress
* Daily performance trend
* Best-performing day
* Lowest-performing day
* Comparison with the previous week
* Unfinished task carryover

Use the user's configured week-start preference.

Default to Monday as the first day of the week.

## C. Monthly analytics

Calculate:

* Total tasks created
* Total tasks completed
* Monthly task completion rate
* Planned versus actual time
* Time distribution by category
* Habit consistency
* Monthly goal progress
* Daily performance trend
* Weekly performance comparison
* Comparison with the previous month
* Overdue task trends

Distinguish between tasks created during the month and tasks completed during the month.

Do not confuse those two metrics.

## D. Yearly analytics

Calculate:

* Total completed tasks
* Total recorded productive hours
* Total scheduled hours
* Annual habit completion rate
* Monthly performance trends
* Annual goal progress
* Most consistent habits
* Most productive categories by tracked time
* Best-performing months
* Comparison with the previous year
* Long-term progress toward selected goals

Use actual historical data.

If previous-year data is unavailable, display an appropriate message rather than inventing a comparison.

## E. Six-month analytics

Provide a six-month overview for users who set half-year goals.

Display monthly progress, goal milestones, habit consistency, tracked hours, and trends across the selected period.

Allow the user to choose the six-month period.

## F. Analytics visualization

Use suitable charts, such as:

* Line charts for trends
* Bar charts for comparisons
* Stacked bars for planned versus actual time
* Donut charts for completion breakdowns
* Heatmaps for habit consistency
* Progress bars for goals
* Calendar views for daily performance

Charts must:

* Use actual data
* Have meaningful labels
* Support empty states
* Display useful tooltips
* Adapt to different screen sizes
* Avoid misleading scales
* Clearly indicate the selected date range

Provide filters for date range, task category, habit, goal, and time category when relevant.

## G. Productivity score

Implement an optional transparent productivity score.

Start with a configurable default formula:

* 40% task completion rate
* 30% habit completion rate
* 30% schedule adherence

The score is:

Productivity Score =
0.40 × Task Completion Rate

* 0.30 × Habit Completion Rate
* 0.30 × Schedule Adherence

Each component is expressed as a value from 0 to 100.

Important rules:

1. Allow the user to configure the weights.
2. Validate that weights are nonnegative and total 100%.
3. Normalize the components to the same 0–100 scale.
4. Define how missing components are handled.
5. Do not treat missing data as successful performance.
6. Clearly label the score as an internal productivity indicator, not an objective measure of personal worth.
7. Display the component breakdown.
8. Allow the user to disable the score and view raw metrics instead.

If there is insufficient data to calculate a meaningful score, display "Insufficient data" rather than a misleading number.

## H. Automated insights

Build a deterministic insight engine using rules and actual metrics.

Examples:

* "Your tracked study time increased by 15% compared with last week."
* "You completed 18 of your 24 planned tasks this week."
* "You are 4 hours behind your monthly study target."
* "Exercise was completed on 6 of its 7 scheduled days."
* "Your task completion rate declined compared with the previous week."

Insights must include their comparison period and use correct arithmetic.

Do not invent reasons for performance changes.

Do not make psychological, medical, or personality diagnoses.

Do not require an external AI service to generate these insights.

